hi I'm Callie plaguey reading this  review of dynasty Warriors 9 for  freelancer James Swain banks dynasty  Warriors 9 represents the biggest shift  away from the series established formula  since moving from a one-on-one fighting  game to its more established Musso form  while the feeling of cutting down entire  formations of soldiers the button press  will feel more than familiar to fans of  the series the introduction of a massive  open world changes the pacing in a way  that allows the action to breathe and  although it suffers from a number of  disappointing technical hitches along  with some typical open-world jenk  dynasty Warriors 9 sprawling campaign  feels right at home in its new setting  given that its story mode is presented  unconventionally it can take a little  while to figure out precisely what's  going on the entire story of the Three  Kingdoms of China is told through the  eyes of over 80 separate playable  characters across four major clans way  wushu and Jin's as well as a handful of  other smaller bit players playing  through each chapter unlocks the next  one along with more characters seeing  each battle from multiple perspectives  is enthralling from a historical point  of view but it can mean playing through  a lot of the same missions multiple  times which can be a little frustrating  given there is little difference in the  feel of any given character out on the  battlefield the open world is the big  game changer here and in so many ways it  works to the game's advantage missions  are picked up from NPCs out in the world  and among the different cities that dot  the landscape instead of out of a menu  although the menu option is still there  if you want to merely move from mission  to Mission traveling from one area to  the other gives you a chance to find  peaceful moments in between each battle  your actions in the open world are also  tied closely to each main quest  completing sub quests will lower the  recommended character level of the main  quest so if you find a mission too  difficult you can polish off a few sub  quests and lower its difficulty if you  make your way off-road when moving  towards the frontlines the chances are  good that you'll find a dangerous group  of bandits to take down or a pack of  wild deer or Tigers to hunt although  many of the optional open-world  activities like hunting or fishing  aren't especially inspiring in  themselves they net you aleut you can  trade for valuable goods or using  to buff your attacking defense the world  is massive and in its own way quite  pretty its sparsity reflects the period  and the vast and varied environments  flows seamlessly into one another the  day/night and full weather cycles aren't  just visual changes but also affect the  action soldiers won't March at nighttime  and bad weather will slow them down but  overall it also just looks plain grimy  at times many of the game's textures  appear as though they've been lathered  in a thick coat of Vaseline cities and  palaces suffer from this the most as  their elaborate architecture often fails  to load with high-res textures at first  leaving them looking like big brown  lumps in the world instead of beautiful  ancient Chinese palaces  aside from randomly appearing geometry  the graphical shortcomings are perhaps  most noticeable in the character  costumes while character models and  costume designs themselves are  absolutely stunning the textures within  them lack the sort of clarity needed to  work up close this combined with rough  animations and some truly abysmal  English voice-over work make sure you  switch to the Chinese voice over  immediately make the story cutscenes a  little tough to look at given the  frequency with which they're shown the  volunteer forces are to assist the  Imperial Army do not try and be a hero  given the game's technical issues it's a  good thing that cutting down thousands  of men in a single setting feels as  satisfying as ever entire squadrons can  be laid to waste in mere moments it's  truly an epic power fantasy that even  after 50 hours of gameplay continues to  thrill while the bow as well as  horseback combat can be underwhelming  the melee combat remains the biggest  draw and the series strongest pillar it  does lack a certain subtle nuance in  some of the later one-on-one boss  battles but nine times out of ten you'll  come out of battle with a smile on your  face  it's clear that Co a tecmo and Omega  Force have gone back to the drawing  board with dynasty Warriors 9 and in  many ways it's a big improvement on the  series mainstays the new open-world  format changes up the game in a way that  helps the flow and pacing of story mode  as well as its core mechanics sure there  are obvious graphical flaws and some  more subtle issues with combat locking  finer controls the streamlining of  countless menus of previous games into  an open world and the laughably fun  moment-to-moment play makes dynasty  Warriors 9 not just a must for fans but  worth a look for them merely curious
 

